# has_enum tail fallback resolves namespaced enum paths the game rejects

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found by the B046 lane, 2026-08-30, while probing the game for module-level
enum resolution. Flagged rather than fixed — deliberately out of B046's scope.

## Defect
`global_scope.rs:302-325` resolves an enum reference by falling back to a
TAIL match on the index key. So `has_enum("Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice")`
returns true because some key ends with `ESceneCharVisNotice`, regardless of
whether the leading namespace path is real.

The game disagrees. Live RemoteBuild probe (Trackmania + Openplanet 1.29.5,
2026-08-30, by the B046 lane):

- `ESceneCharVisNotice` bare, as a type — ACCEPTED
- `ESceneCharVisNotice::DoubleJump` — ACCEPTED
- `Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice` — REJECTED, `Namespace 'Scene' doesn't exist.`
- `Enums::ESceneCharVisNotice` — REJECTED, `Namespace 'Enums' doesn't exist.`

So the LSP accepts qualified paths the game rejects: FALSE NEGATIVES, the
direction where the tool stays silent on code that will not compile. Less
damaging than a false positive, but it is the LSP failing at its actual job.

## Scope — this is NOT specific to module-level enums
The fallback is generic to every enum in the index. B046 merely exposed it:
after B046 lands, `Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice` resolves through this fallback
even though B046 deliberately keys that enum with `namespace: None` precisely
because the game only accepts it bare. The two behaviours will disagree, and
the fallback wins.

Any fix must therefore be measured across ALL enums, not just the two B046
adds. Expect the same shape as B044: a permissive scan that has been masking
its own inaccuracy, where narrowing it turns invisible wrong-accepts into
visible diagnostics that then need explaining.

## Work
- Establish ground truth with a live probe BEFORE changing anything. B044's
  method is the template: enumerate candidate qualified paths across the index,
  submit them to the game, and classify. Do not reason from the dump about what
  the game accepts — B046 showed that reasoning from the header alone produced
  a confidently wrong prediction.
- Then decide whether the fallback narrows, disappears, or becomes
  namespace-aware, and what each option costs in false positives.

## Gate
FP fixture ERROR counts must not RISE. Narrowing this fallback can only ADD
errors, so this is the direction that needs the most care — every new error
must be shown to be a name the game genuinely rejects, not a name the index
merely keys differently. Measure two binaries on the same machine at the same
time (current baselines: 14/1485, 5/276, 2/35, 4/2, 4/4).

## Related
B046 (exposed it; explicitly did not touch it), B044 (the same class of
permissive-scan narrowing, with the probe methodology to copy), B007 (the
ticket the tail fallback was originally written for — check what it needed
before removing anything), B016.
