# Findings (B052 lane, 2026-08-30) — probe first, implement second

## CORRECTION: this ticket's core premise is FALSE

> "The fallback is generic to every enum in the index."

It is not, and the fix should not be reviewed as though it were. `has_enum`'s
fallback calls `lookup_source_enum(tail)`, and that only answers for a
COMPLETE index key. A key with no `::` is the only kind a bare tail can match,
and there are exactly **three** of those among the 630 named enum keys:

    VirtualKey   ESceneVehicleVisEvent   ESceneCharVisNotice

So the fallback's entire surface was `<any prefix>::` + one of those three.
Every other enum is keyed `Module::Class::Enum` or `CoreNs::Enum` and never
reached it. `CGameMenu::EMenuOrder` — the shape the fallback's own B007 comment
claimed to serve — did **not** resolve through it; it resolved through
`resolve_qualified_suffix` in the resolver, and `has_enum` answered false.

Counted with the crate's own loader (`tests/zz_probe_enum_dump.rs`), not a
Python re-derivation.

## What the game actually does

AngelScript resolves `P::T` by looking `T` up in scope `P`, then walking P's
ancestors out to the global namespace. Three outcomes, all probed:

  * `P` is not a scope            -> `Namespace 'P' doesn't exist.`
  * `P` is a scope, `T` unreachable -> `Identifier 'T' is not a data type in
                                       namespace 'P' or parent`
  * `P` is a scope, `T` global    -> ACCEPTED

So deleting the fallback would have been WRONG in the damaging direction: it
would reject `UI::VirtualKey`, `Meta::ESceneCharVisNotice`, `IO::VirtualKey`
and ~100 more spellings the game compiles happily — turning a false negative
into a false POSITIVE.

## What counts as a scope (each half probed, not reasoned)

  * a Core namespace: `UI`, `Meta`, `Json`, `IO`, `nvg`, `UI::Font`, ...
  * an engine CLASS that declares at least one enum, named or anonymous,
    spelled WITHOUT the loader's module prefix. This predicted the game 8/8:
    `CGameMenu`, `CGameCtnApp`, `CGameCtnChallenge` declare enums and
    `X::VirtualKey` compiles; `CSceneVehicleVis`, `CTrackMania`, `CMwNod`,
    `CSystemFidFile`, `Meta::Plugin` declare none and it does not.
  * a namespace the WORKSPACE declares, nested included. Probed with a plugin
    declaring `namespace Foo { namespace Bar { ... } }`: `Foo::VirtualKey` and
    `Foo::Bar::VirtualKey` both compile.
  * every ANCESTOR of any of the above. Declaring `A::B` declares `A`. The
    Core DB names `Internal::NadeoServices` and never `Internal`, so
    `Internal::VirtualKey` was a FALSE POSITIVE until 9d8625b. Same for
    `NSysCfgVision` and `CHmsSolidVisCst_TmCar`. Found by the Codex review
    flagging it as unprobed; probing it proved it real.

NOT a scope: the Nadeo `ns` MODULE names this crate keys the dump under
(`Game`, `Scene`, `Plug`, `System`, `Hms`, `Input`, `Control`, `Function`,
`Graphic`, `Script`, `Vision`, `Xml`, `TrackMania`, `ShootMania`,
`MwFoundations`, `MetaNotPersistent`). They are this crate's invention. Their
module-level enums are global, so they contribute an empty scope path and drop
out by construction rather than by a hardcoded list.

## Probe corpus — committed, verbatim

`docs/probes/b052-qualified-enum-spellings.tsv` — 1901 spellings, each with
the game's own message. 742 accepted, 1159 rejected. Regenerate with
`scripts/probe_enum_corpus.py --b052`. Read `docs/probes/README.md` first.

Sample, verbatim:

    ESceneCharVisNotice                     ACCEPTED
    Scene::ESceneCharVisNotice              REJECTED  Namespace 'Scene' doesn't exist.
    Enums::ESceneCharVisNotice              REJECTED  Namespace 'Enums' doesn't exist.
    Game::CGameMenu::VirtualKey             REJECTED  Namespace 'Game::CGameMenu' doesn't exist.
    CSceneVehicleVis::VirtualKey            REJECTED  Identifier 'VirtualKey' is not a data type in namespace 'CSceneVehicleVis' or parent
    Meta::Plugin::VirtualKey                REJECTED  Identifier 'VirtualKey' is not a data type in namespace 'Plugin' or parent
    UI::VirtualKey                          ACCEPTED
    CGameMenu::VirtualKey                   ACCEPTED
    CGameMenu::EMenuOrder                   ACCEPTED
    Internal::VirtualKey                    ACCEPTED
    CGameHighScore::EMenuOrder              REJECTED  Identifier 'EMenuOrder' is not a data type in namespace 'CGameHighScore' or parent

## Probe lines of my own that were WRONG, recorded so the rest is credible

  * I initially treated `MathX` and `GameData` as engine namespaces because
    `MathX::VirtualKey` and `GameData::VirtualKey` compiled. They are not.
    Both are artefacts of plugins installed on this machine — the same reason
    `MLFeed::VirtualKey` and `VehicleState::VirtualKey` compile here. Excluded
    from the model. This is the ACCEPT-is-not-evidence trap; see
    `docs/probes/README.md`.
  * My first pass at "which enums are globally visible" used "the game accepts
    the bare NAME as a type", which wrongly admitted `ESpectatorCameraType` and
    `EPrestigeMode` — both have a nested enum of the same name as a
    module-level one. Corrected to "key depth <= 1", which predicts 2987 of
    2989 verdicts instead of 2970.
  * The b052 fixture's 3 `string::VirtualKey` lines are a PRE-EXISTING parser
    false positive (the lexer treats `string` as a keyword), not a B052 effect.
    The merge-base binary emits them identically. Pinned in the fixture's own
    `KnownParserGap()` section rather than quietly dropped.

## Not fixed here — filed as B056

1010 qualified spellings the LSP accepts and the game rejects, which are NOT
the tail fallback: exact-key acceptance of the synthetic module prefix
(`Game::CGameCtnApp`), and `resolve_unqualified` rescuing bare nested-enum
names (`EMenuOrder`). Correct for CLASSES, wrong for nested ENUMS.
